Topi Linkala <nes@iki.fi> wrote in news:br4fa2$5kb$1@nyytiset.pp.htv.fi:

> Or we could change add_to_minv() to insert the item in the proper
> place in the minvent chain, so that it's sorted by weight.
> 
> Then we could just drop off the heavier items from the front of the
> chain. 

Given that a monster polymorphing into something with a lower carrying 
capacity is several times less likely than a monster picking something up, 
I think that this would increase the overall computing effort required 
considerably, compared to sorting the list when required to. Particularly 
when you consider that sorting a list after it has been created is quicker 
than adding elements in order as they arrive.

Of course, the effort would also be more evenly distributed over many more 
turns if it was in add_to_minv() (not that I've ever seen 
add_to_minv() [nor want to]), so you'd have a consistently somewhat slower 
nethack, rather than a nethack that may pause for a while whenever 
something polymorphs into something with a lower carrying capacity.

I'm not sure that dropping heaviest things first is the correct strategy 
anyway; IMO, it should rather do something like dropping "things the 
monster considers least worth per unit weight" first. This might give an 
approximation to the kind of strategy a player might adopt in that 
circumstance.


